pt09 adversarial lane, surface: stance-cue clause scoping. Greens the RED
frozen in the previous commit.

`cueFires` replaces bare `.test()` for the four claim-directed cue families:
a cue counts unless a negator sits within three words in front of it, in the
same ground. The window is deliberately short — three words is the distance an
English negator reaches without a parser ("is not true", "does not appear to
support", "nothing here confirms"), and widening it starts catching negations
that belong to a different proposition, which is the failure the
documented-limits block already names and which no cue-list edit can fix.

The guard withholds the cue; it does not invert the label. All four inversions
land on Resembles, the neutral reading, because turning a denial into the
opposite verdict requires knowing WHAT is denied, and that is the parser this
instrument has repeatedly declined to become.

Two things are untouched by construction: a negator that is part of a cue's
own text (`no evidence`, `not always`) still fires that cue, because the guard
reads only what precedes the match; and EVIDENCE_CUES stays a bare
passage-wide test, because citing data is a property of the passage, not a
verdict aimed at a clause — that was v2.6.20's ruling and this does not
reopen it.

Cost, measured: 0 of 1,298 corpus labels move and no score moves; gate floors
191/1.0000/1.0000/0.8529 unchanged; the frozen match-behavior benchmark
including its stanceComposition and documentedLimits blocks is green
untouched. Every negation composition in that fixture carries an asserted
misreading, so all of them route through the parity branch and none reaches
this ladder — which is why the class was invisible.

All 18 suite steps green.

